n  and flowering phenology through the talk  i'm going to show you how i've applied  these same methods  to urban trees and ragweed plants in  detroit  to juniper trees in texas and then i'm  going to tell you a little bit  about my future research plans of  applying similar methods  to trees at continental scales  almost all gymnosperms and at least 10  percent of angiosperms are wind  pollinated  in temperate regions like ithaca this  includes most  trees and lots of important crops too  for these plants understanding airborne  pollen is critical for understanding  gene flow  and this is relevant to research that's  being done now at sips  for example pollination is required for  hemp crops that are being grown for  grain  whereas airborne pollen can interfere  with hemp being grown for cbd  another relevant agricultural issue is  the spread of herbicide resistance genes  across  landscapes similarly  understanding airborne pollen is  important for paleo ecologists that  reconstruct  plant communities using pollen grains  and sediment cores  or fossils pollen also  directly affects human health by  triggering allergic reactions  and an understanding of airborne pollen  is important in all of these examples  and to many examples that i haven't  listed  over the next few days i'm looking  forward to talking with many of you  about this topic and how my research  themes  might apply to your study systems too  that being said for most of today i'm  going to focus on airborne pollen  through the lens of allergies and human  health  pollen allergies are becoming  increasingly common around the world  and rising carbon dioxide concentrations  also cause plants to produce more pollen  and pollen that is more allergenic  and even though pollen allergies are  sometimes dismissed as just a nuisance  they make life miserable for millions of  americans and people around the world  they also have big societal consequences  including millions have lost  school and work days every year and can  also trigger potentially fatal asthma  attacks  most of what we know about airborne  pollen comes from pollen monitoring  stations  there are about 80 of these stations in  the united states which are run through  the national allergy bureau network  and each of these stations takes  integrated daily measurements  here's a map of these monitoring  stations  if we zoom into the northeast you can  see that the nearest station to ithaca  is in rochester about 75 miles away  the current paradigm in this field is to  just use data from the nearest  monitoring station  in other words it is standard practice  to assume that pollen concentrations in  ithaca  are the same as they are on one roof in  rochester  in fact when you see pollen  concentrations reported in the media  what you're usually getting is  yesterday's measurements  likely from tens of miles away from you  also while the forecasting methods that  are used by companies  are usually proprietary one of the  poorly kept secrets is that a lot of  these forecasts from  leading websites are actually based off  of allergy medication  sales which is not always the best proxy  for  airborne pollen concentrations so  even in the best case these predictions  of airborne pollen generally assume that  pollen is homogeneous within regions  surprisingly there's very little data on  how pollen concentrations  vary within regions one potential  explanation for why we don't know more  is that most plant ecologists don't know  much about allergies  and most allergists don't know much  about plants so i think this is kind of  fallen into gaps between fields a little  bit  however there's another really big  reason why we don't know more  and that is the the methods that are  used for sampling pollen  and traditionally two types of samplers  have been used the roto-rod and the  burkhart sampler  these samplers were designed about 70  years ago and haven't changed much since  then  they're bulky they need to be plugged  into ac  power and they also aren't cheap  which makes it difficult to conduct  these large  simultaneous measurement campaigns that  we need  to figure out how pollen varies  over relatively small spatial scales  one of my contributions here was making  an updated design of one of these pollen  sampler types myself  and i was lucky enough to get some help  from a few  engineering grad students on this and i  ended up soldering together a fleet of  these samplers  the designs for them are currently in  prep for submission to an open source  hardware journal  with these samplers i was able to  empirically test whether this large  assumption of pollen being homogeneous  within a region was true  next i'm going to show you some results  on that  this is a map of detroit the study area  for my first postdoc  and this map shows simultaneously  collected oak pollen measurements across  detroit the color of each of these  circles  indicates airborne pollen on a  particular day  so this is just one day as an example  and  i'd like to note that the color scale  here is logarithmic  and the take home message from this is  that pollen concentrations  do vary dramatically over relatively  short  distances  it also shows why using a single station  as a proxy for an entire region  doesn't work all that well in fact  measurements from that pollen station  shown in the upper right hand corner of  this map  they only explain about six percent of  the variation in oak pollen across the  area  across the season so uh and i should  also point out that other taxa had  similar results and concentrations  on a particular day often varied by  two or three orders of magnitude across  the city  to me this means that we've been making  bad assumptions about exposure to  airborne pollen  because we haven't been thinking about  it spatially  i think it's time for a paradigm shift  and our understanding of airborne pollen  in my talk today i'm going to show you  the benefits of transitioning from  a phenomenological monitoring network to  a spatial modeling approach that  includes the underlying ecological  and biological processes i think this  has the potential to  revolutionize our understanding of  allergenic pollen exposure  so in the next section of my talk i'm  going to walk you through my approach to  modeling airborne pollen  which requires answering these questions  first how much pollen does a single  plant produce  second where are the plants that are  releasing pollen  and third when are these plants  flowering  and releasing pollen now each of these  are basic research  questions on their own but the  combination of them leads to  applied solutions to real world problems  and in fact even though this is research  on plants  the health implications here are strong  enough that it was competitive for nih  funding  which supported my postdoc at u of m  so let's dive into pollen production my  goal was to be able to  estimate an individual tree's pollen  production as a function of its size  but no such equations had been developed  for north american tree species  and the equations that had been  developed for trees elsewhere  generally used pretty coarse methods  i set about creating equations for  several tree species  but for most of my talk today i'm going  to show you examples from oak trees  because they are common and produce lots  of allergenic pollen  at this point i'd also like to  acknowledge victoria bankowski who was  an  undergraduate student who worked with me  for two years on a couple of projects  including this one  here's an example of how i estimated  pollen production  first i selected trees of a range of  sizes  and then i borrowed a trick from my days  of assessing understory light  availability  and took hemispherical photos of each  tree's canopy  these photos made it easy for me to  systematically  and quantitatively estimate the number  of flower clusters per tree  i then took branch samples and counted  the number of  catkins per cluster and then counted the  number of anthers per catkin  and finally counted the number of pollen  grains per anther  i then multiplied all of these together  to estimate a tree's pollen production  so the question is how well can we  predict  pollen production based on a tree's size  on the x-axis here is a tree's canopy  area  and on the y-axis is the number of  pollen grains produced  in millions so let's take a look at the  results  the dots here are individual trees and  the lines are regressions  the mean is in blue and the variation  which is based on bootstrapping  is in gray so for red oak trees  larger trees produced more pollen and  they did so  in a predictable fashion  this held true for the other species in  the study too  and it turns out that tree size is  a very good predictor of pollen  production  that being said i want to take a moment  to focus in on something here i've  i have uh pollen production in the  millions but  it's kind of hard to get a good gut  sense of what a hundred million pollen  grains means  so i want to put those numbers in some  context  the largest white mulberry tree that i  measured had about 125 million anthers  here's an animation of a single white  mulberry anther releasing pollen  that single anther has about 50 000  pollen grains in it  but only it only takes as few as 15  pollen grains  per cubic meter of air for the most  sensitized people to have an allergic  reaction  so imagine being allergic to white  mulberry  and having hundreds of these trees in  your city  the next step in developing a model of  airborne pollen was to figure out where  each of these trees were now my study  area  was the city of detroit which is big and  at 360 square kilometers  there's no way to measure all of these  trees by hand  luckily we're living at a time when vast  amounts of new remote sensing data  are becoming available as are processing  techniques to manage it  and these can be applied to tasks like  tree  identification of these new data sources  is a company called near map which takes  high resolution  aerial images of most urban areas in the  us  a few times a year and this data set had  never been used for  tree identification i combined it  the the multi-temporal aerial images  from your map  with a few other data sets including  lidar and a street tree database  and used that to identify trees in  detroit  i'm going to show you a brief overview  of how i did this analysis  but if you have questions about the  details i'd be happy to discuss it more  in the question and answer session after  the talk  okay so here's an aerial image of one  spot in detroit  you can see trees houses a golf course  and now let's overlay that lidar data on  top of this  lidar provides information about the  three-dimensional structure  of things and here the the lighter  colors  the white corresponds to taller objects  with this information about the height  of each object  we can then use segmentation algorithms  to segment segment these into canopies  of individual trees or at least large  piece  pieces of a tree and that's shown by  these yellow polygons here  i then extracted various indices from  the aerial imagery  and the lidar and used that to classify  the trees  the color of each polygon here  corresponds to the predicted taxon of  that tree canopy polygon  this worked out great for several facts  but others i couldn't tease apart as  well  but even with these limitations these  predictions of  individual tree identity are incredibly  useful  i'd also like to note that we're on the  cusp of tree identification with remote  sensing  being operational and over the next few  years these data sets are going to  become  more and more widespread and  accessible now i'd like to draw your  attention to these green polygons which  are oak trees  and here's where it gets really exciting  because we know the area of each of  these tree canopy polygons  we can plug in those allomet